    "body": "ATCC opened its Centennial Biomanufacturing Center in Manassas, Virginia, adding a U.S. site that will make, store and ship biological materials used in medical research and product development. The nonprofit says it invested more than $55 million in the project, with additional funding from federal and state grants.\nWhat’s Related\nThe 50,000-square-foot center is at ATCC’s headquarters in Prince William County’s Innovation Park. The organization, formally known as the American Type Culture Collection, expects the expansion to create up to 75 full-time jobs .\nThe center will also check the identity and properties of the materials it makes before sending them to researchers. ATCC says it built the facility to handle large orders and increase production when demand rises, including during public health emergencies.\nFrom production to shipping\nATCC supplies cell lines and other biological reference materials to government agencies, universities , and companies. Researchers use those materials as a common starting point so they can compare results across labs or repeat an experiment using the same kind of sample.\nWith production and storage at the same site, ATCC can prepare materials for shipment from the facility where they’re made and checked. ATCC says it will use secure handling procedures and temperature-controlled shipping to keep the materials in usable condition during distribution.\n“When public health threats emerge, the nation's response depends on rapid access to trusted biological materials and the infrastructure to produce them at scale,” said Rebecca Bradford, a senior vice president at ATCC. One of the center’s first assignments will be making materials for the National Institutes of Health to use in outbreak research and response.\nThe center will also develop and test lab models that mimic parts of the human body, including the lungs. ATCC says the models could help researchers evaluate potential treatments while relying less on animal studies. The U.S. Department of Agriculture will also use the center to help advance vaccine and treatment research.\nHow the expansion came together\nConstruction began in September 2024, when Virginia announced it had competed with Maryland for the project and approved an $800,000 grant. The state also offered recruitment and training through its Virginia Talent Accelerator Program. ATCC has been based at Innovation Park since 1998 and has research centers in Gaithersburg and Germantown, Maryland.\nFederal funding included a construction grant from the National Institutes of Health and a separate grant from the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases. The latter helped fund the center’s research and pandemic preparedness work.",
